\chapter{Interstitial Pluralism in Role Constellations}
\labch{interstitial}
\margintoc

\begin{chapteroverview}
This chapter develops the concept of \textbf{interstitial pluralism}---the phenomenon where actors occupy in-between positions across multiple role categories simultaneously. We analyze how EIT ecosystem actors navigate plurality, the strategic value of interstitial positioning, and the tensions between specialization and versatility. Building on structural approaches to organizational identity, we introduce the concept of \textit{sociotypes}---fluid relational configurations that emerge from the patterned interconnections among role identities.
\end{chapteroverview}

\begin{quote}
\textit{``The institutional order is experienced as an objective reality. It has a history that antedates the individual's birth and is not accessible to his biographical recollection. It was there before he was born, and it will be there after his death.''}\\
--- Peter L. Berger \& Thomas Luckmann, \textit{The Social Construction of Reality} \cite{berger1967social}
\end{quote}


