\subsection{Structural Properties of Sociotype Constellations}

We analyze sociotype constellations along two primary dimensions: \textit{differentiation} and \textit{interconnectedness}.\sidenote{\textbf{Two dimensions}---parallel classic sociological concerns with division of labor (differentiation) and social integration (interconnectedness).}

\textbf{Differentiation} refers to the extent to which distinct relational configurations are discernible within a constellation. A highly differentiated constellation contains multiple clusters with distinct relational signatures; a less differentiated constellation exhibits fewer or less distinct clusters. Differentiation indicates the variety of relational configurations through which an organization engages its environment---but it does not imply that these configurations are bounded or mutually exclusive.

\textbf{Interconnectedness} refers to the relationships between configurations within a constellation. Configurations may be densely or sparsely connected; connections may be evenly distributed or concentrated at particular interfaces; certain role categories may serve as bridges linking multiple configurations while others remain more localized. Interconnectedness reveals how relational plurality is organized---whether configurations are segmented and compartmentalized or integrated and overlapping.

