Within constellations exhibiting both differentiation and interconnectedness, we attend particularly to \textit{interfaces}---the role categories that connect configurations.\sidenote{\textbf{Interfaces}---structural locations where relational work is most active, as actors navigate between configurations.} We distinguish:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Localized categories}: Role identities or counterroles that appear predominantly within one configuration
    \item \textbf{Bridging categories}: Role identities or counterroles that connect two or more configurations
    \item \textbf{Multivocal categories}: Bridging categories whose evocation leaves open which configuration is being instantiated
\end{itemize}

Multivocal categories are particularly significant for organizations navigating plural environments.\sidenote{\textbf{Multivocality}---the property of a category that allows it to be interpreted through multiple relational frames simultaneously, creating productive ambiguity.} They enable ``robust action'' \cite{padgett1993robust}---the capacity to ``retreat behind a shroud of multiple identities'' and avoid commitment until the situation clarifies which framing will succeed. Multivocal categories create ambiguity that organizations can exploit strategically, satisfying diverse stakeholders through practices that can be read through multiple frames.

Finally, we introduce \textit{sociotype permeability} as an attribute of individual configurations within a constellation.\sidenote{\textbf{Permeability}---describes structural overlap regardless of whether overlap generates conflict or complementarity.} Permeability captures the extent to which a configuration shares role categories with other configurations---the totality of interfaces it maintains. A highly permeable configuration has porous boundaries and extensive overlap with others; a less permeable configuration is more self-contained.


