\subsection{The Knowledge and Innovation Communities}

As of 2024, nine KICs operate under the EIT umbrella (see Table~\ref{tab:kic-dates}). Each KIC comprises a partnership of universities, research organizations, and businesses operating through geographically distributed ``Co-Location Centres'' (CLCs). KICs receive core funding from the EIT (derived from the EU's Horizon Europe programme) supplemented by partner contributions and revenue-generating activities. They are expected to become financially sustainable over a 15-year funding cycle.

KIC activities span three domains corresponding to the knowledge triangle:\sidenote{\textbf{Knowledge triangle}---originates in Nordic innovation policy and was adopted by the EU as a framing for the EIT's mission.}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Education}: Master's and doctoral programmes carrying the ``EIT Label,'' executive education, and entrepreneurship training
    \item \textbf{Research/Innovation}: Collaborative R\&D projects, technology transfer, and proof-of-concept funding
    \item \textbf{Business creation}: Startup acceleration, access to finance, and scale-up support
\end{itemize}

This portfolio requires KICs to maintain relationships with diverse constituencies: students seeking credentials, researchers seeking funding, startups seeking capital, corporations seeking innovation pipelines, universities seeking partnerships, and public authorities seeking policy implementation. Each constituency brings expectations rooted in different relational conventions.


