\section{Methods: Computational Extraction and Structural Analysis}[Methods]
%═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\labsec{interstitial:methods}

\subsection{Overview of Analytical Approach}

Our methodology proceeds in four phases:\sidenote{\textbf{Reproducibility}---code and intermediate outputs are available in the supplementary materials.}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Triple extraction}: Systematic identification of role identity--practice--counterrole triplets from organizational communications
    \item \textbf{Network construction}: Assembly of triplets into a semantic network relating role categories through shared practices
    \item \textbf{Community detection}: Application of clustering methods to identify emergent sociotype configurations
    \item \textbf{Structural analysis}: Characterization of constellation properties including differentiation, interconnectedness, and permeability
\end{enumerate}

We employ natural language processing for triple extraction, combining dependency parsing with large language model-assisted interpretation where syntactic patterns prove insufficient.


\subsection{Data Collection}

\subsubsection{Corpus Construction}

Our primary data source is the public-facing websites of the nine KICs and the EIT itself. Websites serve as the primary vehicle through which KICs communicate with external constituencies and construct public-facing identities. Unlike internal documents, websites address multiple audiences simultaneously and cannot resolve plurality through compartmentalization---different stakeholders access the same materials.

We constructed the corpus through systematic web crawling. For each KIC, we extracted:\sidenote{\textbf{Data sources}---web crawling used standard tools with politeness delays; PDF extraction used PyMuPDF.}

\begin{itemize}
    \item All pages from the main website (typically 200--500 pages per KIC)
    \item Strategic documents and annual reports (PDF extraction)
    \item Programme descriptions and calls for proposals
    \item News items and press releases from the preceding 24 months
\end{itemize}

After removing duplicate content, navigation elements, and pages without substantive text, the corpus comprised approximately 12,000 documents totaling 3.2 million words across nine KICs.


\subsubsection{Sampling and Filtering}

We filtered the corpus to retain only documents containing explicit references to external constituencies. Documents consisting solely of internal organizational information, contact details, or procedural content were excluded.


\subsection{Triple Extraction}

\subsubsection{Preprocessing}

Documents underwent standard preprocessing:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Sentence segmentation
    \item Coreference resolution to link pronouns and nominal references to their antecedents
    \item Named entity recognition to identify organizational actors
    \item Part-of-speech tagging and dependency parsing
\end{itemize}


\subsubsection{Extraction Procedure}

We identify relational triplets through syntactic dependency patterns.\sidenote{\textbf{Dependency parsing}---identifies grammatical relations between words, enabling extraction of subject-verb-object structures.} We extract triplets where:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A subject noun phrase (the KIC or its representative) stands in subject relation to a verb
    \item The verb governs a direct object, prepositional object, or complement denoting an external actor
    \item The verb represents an action or practice rather than a copular or auxiliary construction
\end{itemize}

Extracted triplets take the form:

\begin{equation}
    \tau = \langle \text{subject}, \text{predicate}, \text{object}, \text{source} \rangle
\end{equation}

For complex sentences where dependency parsing proves insufficient, we supplement with language model-assisted extraction, prompting for relational triplets and reconciling outputs through comparison.


\subsubsection{Normalization}

Extracted triplets undergo normalization to enable aggregation:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Subject normalization}: References to the KIC (``we,'' ``EIT Food,'' ``the programme'') are mapped to a canonical KIC identifier
    \item \textbf{Predicate normalization}: Verbs are lemmatized; synonymous actions are clustered through embedding similarity
    \item \textbf{Object normalization}: External actors are mapped to category labels through semantic clustering
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Network Construction}

We represent the corpus of normalized triplets as a weighted two-mode network $G = (V_A, V_P, E)$ where:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $V_A$ is the set of actor nodes (role identities and counterroles)
    \item $V_P$ is the set of practice nodes (normalized predicates)
    \item $E$ is the set of edges connecting actors through shared practices
\end{itemize}

Edge weights reflect the frequency of triplets linking two categories.

For community detection, we project the two-mode network to a one-mode actor network where actors are linked if they share practices:

\begin{equation}
    w_{ij} = \sum_{p \in V_P} \frac{e_{ip} \cdot e_{jp}}{\sqrt{d_i \cdot d_j}}
\end{equation}

where $e_{ip}$ is the weight of the edge between actor $i$ and practice $p$, and $d_i$ is the degree of actor $i$. This normalized projection accounts for varying actor frequencies.


\subsection{Community Detection}

We employ stochastic block models (SBMs) for community detection \cite{peixoto2019bayesian,fortunato2010community}.\sidenote{\textbf{SBMs}---model the network as generated by latent block structure, enabling principled inference about community composition.} SBMs provide a principled probabilistic framework that:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Discovers community structure without requiring a predetermined number of clusters
    \item Handles weighted edges
    \item Provides uncertainty quantification through posterior distributions
    \item Avoids overfitting through Bayesian model selection
\end{itemize}

Crucially, SBMs return \textit{soft assignments}---probability distributions over community membership rather than hard partitions. This preserves the fluidity central to the sociotype concept: a role category may participate in multiple configurations with different intensities rather than belonging exclusively to one.


\subsection{Sociotype Profiling}

For each detected configuration, we construct a rich descriptive profile:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Structural characteristics}: Internal density, external connectivity, bridging nodes, centrality
    \item \textbf{Relational signature}: Characteristic practices (TF-IDF weighted), role identities, and counterroles
    \item \textbf{Textual fingerprint}: Representative triplets, distinguishing vocabulary
    \item \textbf{Cross-KIC distribution}: Prevalence in each KIC's network
    \item \textbf{Boundary characteristics}: Membership entropy, nodes with distributed assignments
\end{itemize}

Profiles enable interpretation while resisting premature categorical closure. We do not assign labels like ``market'' or ``profession'' to detected configurations; instead, we describe their relational signatures and let readers draw connections to existing concepts as appropriate.


\subsection{Measures of Constellation Structure}

\subsubsection{Differentiation}

Differentiation is operationalized as the number of distinct configurations and their relative separation:

\begin{equation}
    D = k \cdot \left(1 - \frac{\bar{s}_{between}}{\bar{s}_{within}}\right)
\end{equation}

where $k$ is the number of configurations, $\bar{s}_{within}$ is mean within-cluster similarity, and $\bar{s}_{between}$ is mean between-cluster similarity.


\subsubsection{Interconnectedness}

Interconnectedness captures the density of cross-configuration connections:

\begin{equation}
    I = \frac{\sum_{i \neq j} w_{ij} \cdot \mathbf{1}[c_i \neq c_j]}{\sum_{i \neq j} w_{ij}}
\end{equation}

where $w_{ij}$ is the edge weight between nodes $i$ and $j$, and $c_i$ is the primary configuration assignment of node $i$.


\subsubsection{Permeability}

Permeability for configuration $s$ is:

\begin{equation}
    P_s = \frac{|B_s|}{|M_s|} \cdot \frac{\sum_{b \in B_s} |S_b|}{k - 1}
\end{equation}

where $|B_s|$ is the number of bridging nodes in configuration $s$, $|M_s|$ is total membership, $|S_b|$ is the number of other configurations that bridge $b$ connects to, and $k$ is the total number of configurations.


