\section{Findings: Sociotype Constellations in EIT Communities}[Findings]
%═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\labsec{interstitial:findings}

\subsection{Aggregate Constellation Structure}

Analysis of the aggregate network (pooling triplets across all KICs) reveals a modular constellation comprising six primary sociotype configurations.\sidenote{\textbf{Six configurations}---the number emerged from the data; we did not impose it.} The constellation exhibits moderate differentiation and substantial interconnectedness, indicating that EIT Communities instantiate a plural relational structure with significant overlap between configurations.


\subsection{Sociotype Profiles}

We present profiles of the six primary configurations, proceeding from highest to lowest permeability. For each, we report structural position, relational signature, and representative triplets. We resist assigning categorical labels, instead describing configurations through their relational characteristics.


\subsubsection{Configuration 1: The Connective Configuration}

\textbf{Structural position}: Highly central; highest permeability; interfaces with all other configurations.

\textbf{Relational signature}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Characteristic practices: \textit{connects}, \textit{brings together}, \textit{facilitates}, \textit{enables collaboration}, \textit{builds}
    \item Role identities: \textit{connector}, \textit{platform}, \textit{facilitator}, \textit{builder}
    \item Counterroles: \textit{partners}, \textit{stakeholders}, \textit{actors}, \textit{members}, \textit{innovators}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Representative triplets}:
\begin{quote}
    \small
    ``EIT Food connects food system innovators across Europe''\\
    ``The KIC brings together leading universities, research centres, and companies''\\
    ``We facilitate collaboration between startups and corporate partners''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Cross-KIC distribution}: Present in all KICs; highest prevalence in EIT Climate-KIC and EIT Digital.

\textbf{Relational character}: This configuration positions the KIC as enabling relationships among diverse actors.\sidenote{\textbf{Connective function}---the configuration functions as the constellation's integrative tissue; its high permeability reflects its bridging function.} It emphasizes relational coordination over transaction. The high permeability reflects the configuration's function as connective tissue---it interfaces with constituencies associated with other configurations precisely because brokerage is its defining practice. The counterrole ``partners'' is notably multivocal, appearing across multiple configurations.


\subsubsection{Configuration 2: The Acceleration Configuration}

\textbf{Structural position}: Central; high permeability; strong interfaces with Configurations 1 and 3.

\textbf{Relational signature}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Characteristic practices: \textit{accelerates}, \textit{funds}, \textit{supports}, \textit{invests in}, \textit{scales}
    \item Role identities: \textit{accelerator}, \textit{investor}, \textit{supporter}, \textit{launcher}
    \item Counterroles: \textit{startups}, \textit{entrepreneurs}, \textit{ventures}, \textit{scale-ups}, \textit{founders}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Representative triplets}:
\begin{quote}
    \small
    ``EIT Health accelerates promising healthcare startups''\\
    ``Our programme supports entrepreneurs from idea to market''\\
    ``The KIC has invested in over 500 ventures since 2015''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Cross-KIC distribution}: Present in all KICs; highest prevalence in EIT Digital and EIT InnoEnergy.

\textbf{Relational character}: This configuration organizes relationships around venture development and growth.\sidenote{\textbf{Acceleration permeability}---reflects the need to connect venture development with research inputs and educated talent.} Role identities and practices map onto established categories of entrepreneurial support. The configuration's permeability reflects the need to connect venture activity with other knowledge triangle vertices---ventures require research inputs (Configuration 4) and educated talent (Configuration 3).


\subsubsection{Configuration 3: The Development Configuration}

\textbf{Structural position}: Moderately central; moderate permeability.

\textbf{Relational signature}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Characteristic practices: \textit{educates}, \textit{trains}, \textit{develops}, \textit{certifies}, \textit{prepares}
    \item Role identities: \textit{educator}, \textit{trainer}, \textit{certifier}, \textit{developer}
    \item Counterroles: \textit{students}, \textit{learners}, \textit{participants}, \textit{talents}, \textit{future leaders}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Representative triplets}:
\begin{quote}
    \small
    ``Our Master's programmes prepare students for innovation careers''\\
    ``EIT Manufacturing trains the workforce of tomorrow''\\
    ``The EIT Label certifies educational excellence in entrepreneurship''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Cross-KIC distribution}: Present in all KICs; highest prevalence in EIT Digital and EIT Health.

\textbf{Relational character}: This configuration organizes relationships around human capability formation.\sidenote{\textbf{EIT Label}---emerges as a distinctive credentialing device linking this configuration to formal certification.} The ``EIT Label'' emerges as a distinctive credential. Moderate permeability reflects the bounded nature of educational relationships---students are addressed through specifically educational practices---while interfaces with Configurations 1 and 2 indicate the entrepreneurial orientation distinguishing EIT education from traditional academic programmes.


\subsubsection{Configuration 4: The Knowledge Configuration}

\textbf{Structural position}: Peripheral; lower permeability.

\textbf{Relational signature}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Characteristic practices: \textit{researches}, \textit{develops}, \textit{discovers}, \textit{advances}, \textit{validates}
    \item Role identities: \textit{researcher}, \textit{knowledge generator}, \textit{scientific partner}
    \item Counterroles: \textit{researchers}, \textit{scientists}, \textit{research institutions}, \textit{universities}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Representative triplets}:
\begin{quote}
    \small
    ``Our research projects advance understanding of sustainable food systems''\\
    ``EIT Climate-KIC partners with leading research institutions''\\
    ``The programme validates innovative technologies through rigorous testing''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Cross-KIC distribution}: Present in all KICs; relatively even distribution.

\textbf{Relational character}: This configuration organizes relationships around knowledge production and validation.\sidenote{\textbf{Knowledge peripherality}---may reflect structural tensions in the EIT model, which emphasizes application over basic research.} Lower permeability and peripheral position reflect a notable pattern: KICs partner with research organizations but rarely position themselves primarily as knowledge generators. The configuration interfaces mainly with Configuration 2 (research inputs to commercialization) rather than standing as an end in itself.


\subsubsection{Configuration 5: The Accountability Configuration}

\textbf{Structural position}: Peripheral; low permeability.

\textbf{Relational signature}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Characteristic practices: \textit{delivers impact}, \textit{reports to}, \textit{contributes to}, \textit{addresses challenges}, \textit{serves}
    \item Role identities: \textit{impact deliverer}, \textit{policy contributor}, \textit{servant}
    \item Counterroles: \textit{European Commission}, \textit{policymakers}, \textit{citizens}, \textit{society}, \textit{Europe}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Representative triplets}:
\begin{quote}
    \small
    ``EIT Food contributes to EU food policy objectives''\\
    ``Our activities deliver measurable impact for European citizens''\\
    ``The KIC addresses societal challenges in sustainable mobility''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Cross-KIC distribution}: Present in all KICs; highest prevalence in EIT Climate-KIC and EIT Food.

\textbf{Relational character}: This configuration reflects the public funding context and associated accountability demands.\sidenote{\textbf{Accountability separation}---echoes findings on ``decoupling''---formal compliance structures disconnected from operational activities.} Its peripheral position and low permeability suggest structural separation from core operational activities---public accountability is acknowledged but not integrated into the relational configurations through which KICs engage direct constituencies.


\subsubsection{Configuration 6: The Sectoral Configuration}

\textbf{Structural position}: Variable by KIC; moderate permeability.

\textbf{Relational signature}: Varies by sectoral domain. Examples:
\begin{itemize}
    \item EIT Health: \textit{improves outcomes for} $\rightarrow$ \textit{patients}; \textit{transforms} $\rightarrow$ \textit{healthcare systems}
    \item EIT Food: \textit{supports} $\rightarrow$ \textit{farmers}; \textit{serves} $\rightarrow$ \textit{consumers}
    \item EIT Urban Mobility: \textit{enables} $\rightarrow$ \textit{cities}; \textit{benefits} $\rightarrow$ \textit{commuters}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Relational character}: This configuration captures domain-specific stakeholder relationships that differentiate KICs from one another. Unlike the preceding configurations (which appear across all KICs), the sectoral configuration varies in content while maintaining structural similarity. It represents the point of contact between the generic innovation intermediary model and specific societal challenges.


\subsection{Interconnectedness and Interfaces}

The aggregate constellation exhibits uneven interconnectedness.\sidenote{\textbf{Topology}---uneven interconnectedness generates the distinctive ``topology'' of relational plurality in each organization.}

Key findings on interconnectedness:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dense interface}: Configurations 1 (Connective) and 2 (Acceleration) share extensive overlap, with ``partners'' and ``innovators'' functioning as multivocal categories evocable from either configuration.
    \item \textbf{Moderate interface}: Configurations 2 (Acceleration) and 3 (Development) connect through categories like ``entrepreneurs'' (who are both investment targets and educational products) and practices like ``develops'' (applicable to both ventures and human capital).
    \item \textbf{Sparse interface}: Configurations 4 (Knowledge) and 5 (Accountability) show minimal direct connection, suggesting structural separation between research-oriented and policy-oriented activities.
    \item \textbf{Bridging function}: Configuration 1 (Connective) provides the primary connection between otherwise separated configurations, consistent with its high permeability and brokerage function.
\end{itemize}

