\section{Conclusion}
%═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\labsec{interstitial:conclusion}

This chapter has developed a structural approach to relational plurality in interstitial organizations.\sidenote{\textbf{Broader ambition}---a \textit{structural sociology of relational plurality} that attends to patterns without reifying them as fixed categories.} Through computational analysis of EIT Knowledge and Innovation Community communications, we have identified the sociotype constellations through which these innovation intermediaries navigate their complex relational environments.

Our findings reveal fluid, modular plurality: differentiated configurations connected through multivocal role categories that function as interfaces and bridges. The Connective configuration emerges as central integrative tissue; Acceleration and Development configurations occupy core positions; Knowledge and Accountability configurations remain more peripheral. This structure enables EIT Communities to maintain recognizable relationships with diverse constituencies while creating spaces for cross-domain integration.

The concept of sociotype---fluid relational configuration defined by structural position rather than essential properties---offers an alternative to categorical approaches.\sidenote{\textbf{Future work}---might examine when and how sociotypes stabilize into more categorical forms, and when they resist such stabilization.} Sociotypes are discovered through pattern detection rather than imposed from theoretical typologies; they blend and recombine rather than existing as discrete containers; their boundaries are empirically variable rather than analytically predetermined.

For practitioners and policymakers, our analysis offers diagnostic value. Understanding the sociotype constellation characterizing an organization---which configurations are present, how they interconnect, where ambiguity enables flexibility and where distinctiveness constrains options---provides insight into the structural conditions of navigating plurality.

The broader ambition is a structural sociology of relational plurality---one that takes seriously the patterned character of plural environments while resisting the temptation to treat patterns as fixed, bounded, and inherently conflictual.\sidenote{\textbf{Constitution}---organizations do not merely \textit{respond to} plurality; they \textit{constitute} it through the relational configurations they enact.} Organizations do not merely respond to plurality; they constitute it through the relational configurations they enact. Understanding those configurations, their structural properties, and their dynamic evolution is essential to understanding contemporary organizational life.


